### Using the function "str_mean_estimate_data"

Instead of pre-calculating the summary stats, this function extracts everything directly from the dataset using the pre-assigned sampling weights.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
## In the function "str_mean_estimate_data", we can find the stratum size with the variable "weights":
tapply(agstrat$weight, agstrat$region, sum)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
  NC   NE    S    W 
1054  220 1382  422 
```


:::
:::

**Output Note:** This reconstructs the total population sizes ($N_h$) per region.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
## if the dataset contains a variable "weight"
res_agstrat_acres92 <- str_mean_estimate_data(agstrat, y="acres92", stratum="region", weight="weight", estimate = "mean")
res_agstrat_acres92$table
```

